Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

I am attempting to estimate marsh bird abundance using the generalized multinomial mixture model (gmultmix() in unmarked). I am utilizing point count surveys (n=262) with a passive listening period of 5 1-minute intervals (i.e. secondary sampling periods). Individual detections were recorded for each minute interval. Each point was surveyed 3 times (i.e. 3 primary periods) across 2 years.

After goodness of fit tests using parametric bootstrapping, my best models reveal slight overdispersion (c-hat = ~1.5) but what appears to be lack of fit with both the Poisson and negative binomial (see Expected vs Observed plot below). I think this plot is also showing that abundance is being underestimated?

After reading the AHM books, searching through this group's posts, and talking with others, I understand I have the option of trying different distributions or accounting for overdispersion through adjustment with the c-hat value (i.e., inflating SEs). However, even if I am able to adjust for the overdispersion, I still have the problem of a potentially inaccurate total abundance prediction. In addition, the negative binomial only improves model fit slightly. I've explored the ZIP distribution as well with the simpler binomial n-mix model (pcount()), but it doesn't perform better than the NB.

Does anyone have any other recommendations on how I might proceed with this model approach? I've tried transformations as well, but nothing seems to improve the observed vs expected plots greatly.

Best,

Rachel Anderson
Graduate Research Assistant
M.Sc. Student
Department of Wildlife, Fisheries & Aquaculture
Mississippi State University Coastal Research & Extension Center

